          ],
          "level": 1,
          "content": "6.891 Midterm Examination"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.454,
            0.296,
            0.541,
            0.319
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "3/21/01"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.178,
            0.361,
            0.323,
            0.384
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "1 Search"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.175,
            0.399,
            0.818,
            0.436
          ],
          "content": "Consider the following graph, in which A is the start node and F is the goal node. Assume that nodes are visited at most once."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.206,
            0.449,
            0.564,
            0.659
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.2,
                0.679,
                0.696,
                0.698
              ],
              "content": "1. In what order does uniform-cost search visit the nodes?"
            }
          ],
          "image_source": "images/page_0_image_4.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.493,
            0.874,
            0.503,
            0.888
          ],
          "content": "1"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_idx": 1,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.223,
            0.16,
            0.817,
            0.197
          ],
          "content": "Let the heuristic function  $h(n)$  be the minimum number of arcs between node n and the goal node."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.199,
            0.209,
            0.644,
            0.228
          ],
          "content": "2. Is this an admissible heuristic? Why or why not?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.199,
            0.331,
            0.818,
            0.368
          ],
          "content": "3. In what order does  $A^{*}$  search visit the nodes? What are their estimated values when they are visited?"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.178,
            0.485,
            0.529,
            0.509
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "2 Clausal Normal Form"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.176,
            0.524,
            0.514,
            0.542
          ],
          "content": "Convert the following sentence to CNF."
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.405,
            0.559,
            0.589,
            0.58
          ],
          "content": "(A \\land B) \\lor \\neg (C \\to D)",
          "image_source": "images/page_1_equation_5.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.177,
            0.73,
            0.414,
            0.753
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "3 True or False"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.2,
            0.768,
            0.61,
            0.786
          ],
          "content": "1. All sentences are either valid or unsatisfiable."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.199,
            0.799,
            0.819,
            0.835
          ],
          "content": "2. Iterative-deepening search requires space linear in the depth of the solution."
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.492,
            0.874,
            0.505,
            0.888
          ],
          "content": "2"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_idx": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.199,
            0.16,
            0.778,
            0.179
          ],
          "content": "3. If $A \\models B$, then $A$ is true in all interpretations in which $B$ is true."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.199,
            0.189,
            0.818,
            0.226
          ],
          "content": "4. Testing the validity of a sentence in first-order logic can be done in time exponential in the size of the sentence."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.199,
            0.236,
            0.817,
            0.255
          ],
          "content": "5. DPLL runs in worst-case polynomial time in the length of the sentence."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.176,
            0.281,
            0.474,
            0.305
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "4 First Order Logic"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.175,
            0.32,
            0.819,
            0.375
          ],
          "content": "Match the formula to the English sentence it encodes (note that there are more English sentences than formulas). Let  $Big(x)$  mean “x is big” (so  $\\neg Big(x)$  means “x is small”) and let  $Cat(x)$  mean “x is a cat.”"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.215,
            0.392,
            0.622,
            0.41
          ],
          "content": "1. $\\exists x.Big(x)\\wedge Cat(x)$ A. All cats are big."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.215,
            0.411,
            0.748,
            0.428
          ],
          "content": "2. $\\exists x.Big(x)\\to Cat(x)$ B. There is a small thing or a cat."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.215,
            0.429,
            0.68,
            0.446
          ],
          "content": "3. $\\exists x.Big(x)\\lor Cat(x)$ C. All big things are cats."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.215,
            0.447,
            0.684,
            0.465
          ],
          "content": "4. $\\forall x.Big(x)\\wedge Cat(x)$ D. Everything is a big cat."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.215,
            0.465,
            0.641,
            0.482
          ],
          "content": "5. $\\forall x.Big(x)\\to Cat(x)\\quad \\mathrm{E}.$ All cats are small."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.215,
            0.483,
            0.642,
            0.501
          ],
          "content": "6. $\\forall x.Big(x)\\lor Cat(x)$ F. There is a big cat."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.442,
            0.502,
            0.698,
            0.519
          ],
          "content": "G. All small things are cats."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.442,
            0.52,
            0.729,
            0.538
          ],
          "content": "H. There is a big thing or a cat."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.177,
            0.559,
            0.561,
            0.581
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "5 Resolution-Refutation 1"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.177,
            0.596,
            0.762,
            0.615
          ],
          "content": "Use resolution-refutation to show that the following sentence is valid."
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.408,
            0.626,
            0.587,
            0.645
          ],
          "content": "(P \\to Q) \\lor (Q \\to P)",
          "image_source": "images/page_2_equation_15.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.492,
            0.874,
            0.505,
            0.889
          ],
          "content": "3"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_idx": 3,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.177,
            0.156,
            0.383,
            0.177
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "6 Unification"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.175,
            0.193,
            0.819,
            0.248
          ],
          "content": "Show the most general unifier (MGU) for each pair of sentences below. Also show the result of applying the MGU to the sentences. (Capital letters are constants, lowercase are variables.)"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.2,
            0.264,
            0.443,
            0.283
          ],
          "content": "1. $P(x,x,A)$ and $P(z,B,y)$."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.199,
            0.34,
            0.53,
            0.359
          ],
          "content": "2. $P(F(x), A, G(x))$ and $P(F(z), z, w)$."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.177,
            0.432,
            0.584,
            0.454
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "7 First Order Clausal Form"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.176,
            0.47,
            0.584,
            0.487
          ],
          "content": "Convert the following sentences to clausal form."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.2,
            0.504,
            0.417,
            0.523
          ],
          "content": "1. $\\exists x.\\forall y.K(y,x)\\to D(y)$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.199,
            0.58,
            0.375,
            0.599
          ],
          "content": "2. $\\neg \\exists x.H(x)\\land C(x)$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.2,
            0.656,
            0.362,
            0.675
          ],
          "content": "3. $\\exists x.H(x)\\wedge C(x)$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.201,
            0.733,
            0.353,
            0.751
          ],
          "content": "4. $\\neg \\exists x.\\forall y.L(y,x)$"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.491,
            0.875,
            0.505,
            0.888
          ],
          "content": "4"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_idx": 4,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.176,
            0.155,
            0.564,
            0.178
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "8 Resolution-Refutation 2"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.176,
            0.193,
            0.818,
            0.23
          ],
          "content": "Use resolution-refutation to prove a contradiction from the following sentences. Show the two lines you are resolving and the MGU at each step."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.201,
            0.245,
            0.384,
            0.265
          ],
          "content": "1. $P(x)\\lor Q(F(x),x)$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.2,
            0.276,
            0.363,
            0.296
          ],
          "content": "2. $R(y)\\lor \\neg Q(y,z)$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.201,
            0.306,
            0.318,
            0.326
          ],
          "content": "3. $\\neg R(F(A))$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.201,
            0.338,
            0.288,
            0.358
          ],
          "content": "4.  $\\neg P(A)$"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.176,
            0.674,
            0.511,
            0.696
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "9 The Frame Problem"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.175,
            0.711,
            0.819,
            0.748
          ],
          "content": "Why don't we need the equivalent of frame axioms in the STRIPS planning representation?"
        },
        {
